Sheets: saving a workbook with many text cells as .xlsx/.xls finds shared strings and cell fonts by hash and keeps one copy of each text.
Profiles of saving a 50 MB workbook (380 000 rows, 14 text columns) showed three costs that grew with the number
of text cells (sc/source/filter/excel):
- XclExpSstImpl kept the shared string table in 2048 buckets of sorted vectors keyed by a 16-bit hash. With a
  million different texts every bucket held hundreds of entries, so each new text moved half a bucket and was
  compared a dozen times (XclExpString::IsLessThan alone was 17 % of the save). The table now finds texts in a
  hash index over the whole text and its format runs, compared with XclExpString::IsEqual: the same texts get
  the same numbers in the same order.
- Every text cell built its Excel font from its cell format (font name, size, colour, ...) and looked it up in
  the font list (19 % of the save). A font that is already in the list is only found there again, so the index
  is now kept per cell format and script for the export; formats that are not registered are not cached.
- Every text cell kept its own copy of its text after the table had an equal one. It now shares the table's
  copy, which removes one string object per repeated text from the save's memory.
The logic lives in the new file sc/source/filter/inc/hstxlsexportcache.hxx; the exported files are unchanged
(compared part by part before/after on the large-file fixtures; CppunitTest_sc_subsequent_export_test*).
